WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.040
Here's a riddle: What do greeting cards, family&nbsp;
secrets, and a nosy neighbor have in common?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.720
You don't have to solve the puzzle –&nbsp;
we'll just tell you. "The Batman" is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.320
littered with small details that only&nbsp;
the most dedicated fans could spot.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.760
The movie's version of the Riddler takes&nbsp;
after the Zodiac killer in more ways than one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.520
Not only does he don a terrifying mask&nbsp;
he wears when he targets his victims,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.680
but he also leaves behind puzzles and&nbsp;
ciphers for the detectives to solve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.360
Specifically, he leaves behind&nbsp;
greeting cards for Batman himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:33.840
and inside contain clues that help reveal what&nbsp;
it is he's actually looking for in all of this.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.520
However, fans will want to pay&nbsp;
attention to the outside of those cards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.120
They may not mean much to the characters&nbsp;
in the film, but fans should recognize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.760
that each card contains a reference to a&nbsp;
different Batman adversary. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:51.200
the first card we see features an owl. It may&nbsp;
come across as a spooky Halloween card initially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.040
but fans should recognize the correlation&nbsp;
to the Court of Owls from the comics.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:59.920
That's not the only reference hidden in the&nbsp;
cards. The second one has a Hugo Strange-like mad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:05.280
scientist. The third features a beautiful woman&nbsp;
who looks an awful lot like Poison Ivy. The fourth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:10.320
has a set of eyes reminiscent of the Cheshire&nbsp;
Cat, hinting toward Mad Hatter. And the fifth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.520
card includes a boy with a puppet in an obvious&nbsp;
reference to Scarface and the Ventriloquist.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.720
Each card subtly references a&nbsp;
different Caped Crusader villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.280
and honestly, any of them would make&nbsp;
for a terrifying foe for "The Batman 2."

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.080
"The Batman" differs from previous Batman&nbsp;
films by not necessarily depicting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.880
murder of Thomas and Martha Wayne. Instead,&nbsp;
their deaths are merely alluded to and end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.520
up playing a significant role in the cover-up&nbsp;
Riddler wants to expose. During one sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.400
featuring heavy exposition, we learn the truth&nbsp;
behind the two most prominent families to ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:47.360
grace Gotham — the Waynes and the Arkhams. We&nbsp;
learn that during Thomas Wayne's run for mayor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.480
a reporter threatened to expose Martha's time&nbsp;
being institutionalized in Arkham Asylum.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.760
Martha hasn't been related to the&nbsp;
Arkhams in any of the movies before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:01.360
so this is something of a reference to the Earth&nbsp;
One storyline where her last name is Arkham before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.720
marrying Thomas Wayne. And that's not the&nbsp;
only familial connection the movie makes ...

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:12.000
Even casual moviegoers know who Batman's parents&nbsp;
are, but Selina Kyle's lineage is more up in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.800
air. While it's gone through some changes over&nbsp;
the years, "The Batman" decides to borrow elements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:22.320
from "The Long Halloween" and "Dark Victory,"&nbsp;
making Catwoman's true father Carmine Falcone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.320
The film establishes that her mother,&nbsp;
Maria Kyle, died a long time ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:30.320
and she wants to get close enough to Falcone&nbsp;
to kill him for what he did to her mom.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:34.560
In the "Long Halloween" and "Dark Victory"&nbsp;
comics, the relationship between Selina and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.840
Carmine is far more circumstantial. While she&nbsp;
has a strong hunch that Carmine is her father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.920
she doesn't have any definitive proof.&nbsp;
She's far more sure of herself in the movie.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:48.880
Prior to the release of "The Batman," a box&nbsp;
set collection came out gathering Dark Knight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:53.760
stories that influenced the latest feature&nbsp;
film. Among them was "Batman: Year One,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:57.840
even though director Matt Reeves explained&nbsp;
that the movie wouldn't be an origin story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.160
like we've seen in the past. Still, as&nbsp;
one of the most definitive Batman stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:07.200
it's understandable that the book would influence&nbsp;
the first chapter of a new Batman story. And you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.600
can see that in how the movie recreates one of&nbsp;
the most significant scenes in that storyline.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:15.600
In "Year One," Catwoman scratches&nbsp;
a side of Carmine Falcone's face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:19.040
resulting in claw scars remaining&nbsp;
a fixture of his character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.120
A similar scene occurs in "The Batman"&nbsp;
when Catwoman claws at Carmine's face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:27.440
with her extra long nails. He sports that look&nbsp;
throughout the rest of his time in "The Batman."

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:31.680
Most moviegoers should have a&nbsp;
passing familiarity with the Riddler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.880
and probably know his true&nbsp;
identity as Edward Nygma.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:38.720
This has been his real name in the&nbsp;
likes of "Batman Forever" and "Gotham."

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:44.160
In true comic book fashion, the name shortens to&nbsp;
"E. Nygma," or "enigma," which is appropriate for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.280
a man who loves puzzles and riddles. However,&nbsp;
for some, the name might be a bit too cartoony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:54.400
for more serious fare, which is why this new&nbsp;
version of the character has the name of "Edward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.720
Nashton." As it happens, though, there's a&nbsp;
comic book precedent for even this change.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.800
In the comic books post-"Crisis on Infinite&nbsp;
Earths," it's established that Riddler's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:07.440
birth name is Edward Nashton. That allows the&nbsp;
character to have a more grounded persona,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.960
but in true comic book form, he changes his last&nbsp;
name to "Nygma" to better fit his new persona.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:17.440
It's not hard to figure out why Matt Reeves went&nbsp;
with "Nashton" over "Nygma." The film takes on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.400
the tone of a dark noir film, so a name like&nbsp;
"E. Nygma" probably wouldn't work for the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:27.520
realistic feel of the movie. Still, Reeves managed&nbsp;
to squeeze in a nod to Riddler's alternate names&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:32.000
throughout his comic book continuity. When&nbsp;
Riddler's apprehended at the diner, the police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.960
find two alternate I.D.'s on his person, hinting&nbsp;
at the various names he'd held over the years.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:41.520
Another major moment pulled straight from&nbsp;
the comics occurs during the beginning of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:46.320
the film's final act. As Batman confronts&nbsp;
Riddler, who's locked away in Arkham Asylum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:51.120
he reveals that the machinations of his&nbsp;
grand final plan are already well underway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:55.680
In this instance, "The Batman" finale takes&nbsp;
place in a Gotham that's been flooded by water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:01.360
after various flood walls break apart in an&nbsp;
explosion set forth by Riddler and his henchmen.

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:05.360
The plan should be familiar to anyone&nbsp;
who read the "Zero Year" comic book arc.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:08.160
In that storyline, Riddler&nbsp;
manages to do the same thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:11.280
flooding Gotham and turning it&nbsp;
into an apocalyptic wasteland.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:17.280
An intriguing development occurs during the big&nbsp;
climactic final battle between Batman and Catwoman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:21.920
versus Riddler's henchmen. At a certain&nbsp;
point, Batman becomes too overpowered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:26.640
To help him get back on his feet, Batman&nbsp;
takes out a vial containing a green substance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:31.360
which he uses to inject himself. Suddenly, he&nbsp;
receives the strength to get back on his feet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:34.240
and pummel the final Riddler&nbsp;
henchman into submission.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:38.400
It might take some fans by surprise,&nbsp;
especially seeing how the vial wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:42.800
set up earlier in the film. However, fans&nbsp;
of the comics may recognize the substance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:47.680
as having similar properties to Venom, which is&nbsp;
what Bane uses to give himself super-strength.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:54.680
"And now I add my super soldier&nbsp;
serum, codenamed Venom."

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:00.960
After all, like in the comics, it does appear&nbsp;
to give Batman a boost in energy and strength.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:05.520
It also vastly increases his hostility,&nbsp;
as he goes way over the limit beating up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:09.200
the thug to the point where the Gotham&nbsp;
P.D. have to pull him off. That level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:13.280
of savagery could also be attributed to&nbsp;
what we've seen out of Venom in the past.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:16.720
Could this mean that Bane could&nbsp;
appear in a subsequent "Batman" movie?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:20.000
Similar to Riddler's greeting cards, it&nbsp;
could just be a fun Easter egg to hint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:23.440
at a larger world out there. After&nbsp;
all, the universe of "The Batman"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:29.280
will be further explored in an HBO Max TV&nbsp;
series centered on Gotham P.D. and Penguin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:32.880
so there are plenty of avenues for Bane&nbsp;
to make his way into this franchise.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:36.880
By the end of the film, Batman has&nbsp;
stopped the Riddler's reign of terror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:40.240
and put him safely behind bars&nbsp;
at Arkham Asylum. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:44.640
there's someone in a cell next to Riddler's who&nbsp;
manages to put a smile on that face once again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:48.640
While they're never mentioned by name,&nbsp;
we can make a pretty educated guess.

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:52.480
Riddler's neighbor makes a reference to how one&nbsp;
day a person can feel like they're on top of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:57.200
world, but the next day, the world sees them&nbsp;
as a clown. Then he begins laughing maniacally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:01.920
in such a manner that gets Riddler chuckling,&nbsp;
too. It's easy to put two and two together and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:07.680
surmise that this individual, played by Barry&nbsp;
Keoghan, will be the latest iteration of Joker.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:12.320
In the credits, this character is listed as&nbsp;
"Unseen Arkham Prisoner," so it does give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:16.960
those working behind the scenes some leeway as to&nbsp;
whether they want to pursue a Joker storyline for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:21.440
a future installment of "The Batman" series.&nbsp;
After all, when asked about who he'd like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:26.560
include in a sequel, Matt Reeves surprisingly&nbsp;
picked Mr. Freeze. Still, it's exciting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:30.720
think that Joker is out there somewhere,&nbsp;
just waiting to bring some joy to Gotham.

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:40.800
[laughing]

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:45.120
For years, Warner Bros. tried to get a shared&nbsp;
cinematic universe up and running with the DC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:48.800
Extended Universe. This established&nbsp;
that films like "Man of Steel,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:54.080
"Wonder Woman," and "Aquaman" all existed within&nbsp;
the same timeline, similar to what Marvel had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:58.960
accomplished with the MCU. Over the years, the&nbsp;
franchise has been met with mixed responses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:04.160
so DC has branched off into other separate&nbsp;
entities since the beginnings of the DCEU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:08.480
"The Batman" falls into that category –&nbsp;
it exists in its own separate timeline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:12.640
completely detached from what Ben Affleck had&nbsp;
done during his tenure as the Dark Knight.

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:14.820
"She with you?"

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:16.960
"I thought she was with you."

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:21.040
As such, there aren't too many&nbsp;
references to other DC properties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:24.960
outside of the Batman mythos. However,&nbsp;
it does manage to accomplish a bit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:29.920
world-building that could easily pay off down&nbsp;
the line. Toward the end of the flick, Catwoman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:34.400
mentions how she's going to get out of Gotham&nbsp;
for a while and perhaps head up to Blüdhaven.

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:38.080
The name should sound familiar to anyone&nbsp;
who's read the Batman comics or checked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:43.600
out "Arrow" on The CW. The fictional city has&nbsp;
come up frequently in DC Comics over the years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:49.040
as a nearby locale to Gotham. In fact, when&nbsp;
Nightwing decides to venture out as his own hero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:53.200
he sets up his base of operations in&nbsp;
Blüdhaven to get away from Batman.

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:57.040
What does this mean for the future of "The&nbsp;
Batman?" It might mean nothing and serve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:01.120
as solely a way to get Catwoman out of the&nbsp;
picture to make room for other characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:05.200
in "The Batman 2." But it's good to know&nbsp;
that if Dick Grayson ever enters the fold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:08.320
Blüdhaven is there awaiting its protector.

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:13.040
It's become commonplace for superhero movies&nbsp;
to contain mid- or post-credits scenes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:16.480
Fans have come to anticipate a fun&nbsp;
joke or a teaser of what's to come.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:24.160
"You're still here. It's over. Go home."

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:28.880
No doubt plenty of theatergoers will&nbsp;
wait around until the credits have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:32.400
finished to see if "The Batman" has&nbsp;
anything worth sticking around for.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:35.680
While there's no teaser scene at the&nbsp;
very end of "The Batman," it does show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:38.000
a blink-and-you'll-miss-it tag of a web address.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:41.280
Viewers will remember this is the&nbsp;
URL Batman and Gordon had to visit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:45.360
about halfway through the movie when they&nbsp;
realize Riddler gave them a web address.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:48.240
As it turns out, you can actually visit this URL.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:52.160
It will take you to a website where you'll&nbsp;
encounter some of Riddler's puzzles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:56.080
attempting to answer them to reveal the&nbsp;
truth. It appears the riddles are different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:00.320
each time you visit the site, but one of&nbsp;
the trick questions you'll encounter is,

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:05.680
"From birth to death. From boy to man. All things&nbsp;
change, but this is one thing he will always be."

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:10.720
And don't think you can get any hints on other&nbsp;
websites. If you click away from the page,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:12.800
you're greeted with a message reading,

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:16.480
"You scour the internet for the truth,&nbsp;
but you still need to answer me."

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:20.880
What does it all lead to? We won't spoil the&nbsp;
fun of you figuring out the riddle on your own.

